1
00:00:03,220 --> 00:00:06,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, there were mostly something for me to do.

2
00:00:07,503 --> 00:00:10,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, okay, this needs improvement, this needs improvement.

3
00:00:10,725 --> 00:00:27,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, at the moment, my hold on sensuality, um, can, like, improve by, um, by maybe

4
00:00:38,844 --> 00:00:39,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I'm restraining.

5
00:00:40,105 --> 00:00:42,687
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm restraining, even when it comes to thoughts.

6
00:00:43,708 --> 00:00:53,438
[SPEAKER_01]: And, okay, I can do this until I'm like, you know, it's it's where I'm very steady in it.

7
00:00:54,679 --> 00:00:55,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Then there's...

8
00:00:56,520 --> 00:00:57,842
[SPEAKER_01]: I see certain

9
00:00:59,855 --> 00:01:08,160
[SPEAKER_01]: I was at the... Irritation?

10
00:01:08,541 --> 00:01:19,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, irritations, thoughts of cruelty, sort of like finding faults in different things, different people.

11
00:01:21,089 --> 00:01:25,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Those things I can improve on, but it's just that I'm like...

12
00:01:26,802 --> 00:01:32,306
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm finding less and less, sort of, directions for me to... like, what am I going to do next, sort of thing.

13
00:01:34,867 --> 00:01:40,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you... you're asking what are you going to do next, when you haven't even completed what you're supposed to do now.

14
00:01:41,071 --> 00:01:41,311
Okay.

15
00:01:42,672 --> 00:01:46,054
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what you should do next is, eh... be patient.

16
00:01:48,696 --> 00:01:53,099
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, what you should do right now, and next, and next, and next.

17
00:01:54,477 --> 00:01:59,499
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you're trying to figure out what to do next, while you still know these things can be improved on.

18
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:02,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Why... why would you do that?

19
00:02:03,561 --> 00:02:06,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would you accept that thought and that line of reasoning?

20
00:02:10,492 --> 00:02:12,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you might wonder, well, what will I do next?

21
00:02:12,932 --> 00:02:14,353
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's obviously not the issue here.

22
00:02:14,833 --> 00:02:16,273
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's frustration, sort of.

23
00:02:16,433 --> 00:02:26,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly, which means, oh, so now you're wondering what to do next, while in reality the only reason you're wondering what to do next is because you still haven't done what you're supposed to do now.

24
00:02:26,735 --> 00:02:31,896
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're frustrated with it, so you want to just have it the next step as well, in the meantime.

25
00:02:33,737 --> 00:02:34,777
[SPEAKER_00]: And it doesn't work like that.

26
00:02:38,406 --> 00:02:42,410
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you were to figure out what to do next, it won't be what to do next.

27
00:02:42,450 --> 00:02:46,214
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be simply to appease that frustration or lack of patience.

28
00:02:49,717 --> 00:02:52,000
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the question is, well, why are you impatient?

29
00:02:53,641 --> 00:02:57,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is sitting without doing anything, why is that unpleasant?

30
00:03:04,950 --> 00:03:05,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you looking past it?

31
00:03:06,070 --> 00:03:08,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you not seeing it as something... Oh, wait a minute.

32
00:03:09,451 --> 00:03:12,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, oh, this is what I can improve on, this is what I can deal with.

33
00:03:12,772 --> 00:03:13,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's all fine.

34
00:03:14,333 --> 00:03:15,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what do I do next then?

35
00:03:15,813 --> 00:03:16,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, wait.

36
00:03:17,673 --> 00:03:19,854
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I even do before I improve on these things?

37
00:03:21,915 --> 00:03:29,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why I find it unpleasant to sit alone and not have anything to do.

38
00:03:30,137 --> 00:03:30,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that an issue?

39
00:03:35,598 --> 00:03:42,785
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what you do next is basically what you need to do first.

40
00:03:42,825 --> 00:03:44,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Not forget your situation.

41
00:03:59,612 --> 00:04:04,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, when you have thoughts of sensuality, you're restraining that, because that's what the situation is at the time.

42
00:04:04,937 --> 00:04:08,820
[SPEAKER_00]: When you have thoughts of cruelty or ill-will, you're restraining that, because that's what the situation is at the time.

43
00:04:09,321 --> 00:04:21,572
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, when you have thoughts of neither sensuality nor cruelty, but kind of, you know, pressure of boredom or lack of activity or something unpleasant, well, then you find something to do, instead of dealing with that.

44
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:30,381
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I doing to deal with that?

45
00:04:30,941 --> 00:04:31,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's the thing.

46
00:04:31,902 --> 00:04:33,843
[SPEAKER_00]: You shouldn't basically be doing anything to deal with it.

47
00:04:34,343 --> 00:04:37,325
[SPEAKER_00]: You should be learning how to endure the neutral feeling, because it's not unpleasant.

48
00:04:38,506 --> 00:04:41,968
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason neutral feeling is unpleasant is because you keep refusing to acknowledge it.

49
00:04:43,930 --> 00:04:44,690
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point.

50
00:04:47,592 --> 00:04:54,617
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even like the pain of boredom, even anxiety and fear of boredom, it's only so because you're refusing to acknowledge it, because you're looking past it.

51
00:05:02,026 --> 00:05:08,654
[SPEAKER_01]: That's pretty much where the issue is, where, yeah, I'm sort of pressured.

52
00:05:10,596 --> 00:05:11,577
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not doing anything.

53
00:05:11,798 --> 00:05:12,498
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just sitting.

54
00:05:12,919 --> 00:05:13,460
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm pressured.

55
00:05:15,722 --> 00:05:19,126
[SPEAKER_01]: But, what do I do next?

56
00:05:20,208 --> 00:05:21,990
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a thought that I shouldn't really entertain.

57
00:05:23,422 --> 00:05:25,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's a thought, yeah, yeah, pretty much.

58
00:05:26,483 --> 00:05:30,084
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a thought that you will have to arise, because you've been culted.

59
00:05:30,484 --> 00:05:32,905
[SPEAKER_00]: The whole life revolves around figuring out what to do next.

60
00:05:33,865 --> 00:05:39,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Either what sensuality to pursue next, what distraction to pursue next, what problem to avoid next.

61
00:05:39,647 --> 00:05:40,587
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what people's life is.

62
00:05:40,607 --> 00:05:44,008
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why they always need that activity to kind of enable them to keep floating.

63
00:05:44,428 --> 00:05:46,209
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why everybody is afraid of solitude.

64
00:05:47,429 --> 00:05:50,150
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people say you need to learn how to find pleasure in solitude.

65
00:05:51,928 --> 00:05:53,729
[SPEAKER_00]: When there is nothing to do, like that.

66
00:05:56,331 --> 00:06:00,174
[SPEAKER_00]: So, until that happens, you will have thoughts of, eh... what do I do next?

67
00:06:00,454 --> 00:06:09,020
[SPEAKER_00]: But you also, same like with sensuality, you have an option whether to entertain those thoughts or to endure that state of what do I do next without giving in to define what I do next.

68
00:06:10,161 --> 00:06:13,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what I do next is endure the thoughts of what do I do next.

69
00:06:13,663 --> 00:06:15,345
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you do right now and next.

70
00:06:17,646 --> 00:06:20,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Same principle, just in regard to different things.

71
00:06:25,384 --> 00:06:30,905
[SPEAKER_01]: I think, like, sometimes, like, maybe I should try to establish the body.

72
00:06:31,386 --> 00:06:35,227
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm like, really?

73
00:06:35,307 --> 00:06:37,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, should I do that?

74
00:06:37,747 --> 00:06:38,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to do that?

75
00:06:38,487 --> 00:06:43,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, I'm trying to find an answer to this.

76
00:06:49,396 --> 00:07:05,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, again, as we said before, it's not about what you should do.

77
00:07:07,925 --> 00:07:08,865
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about what you won't do.

78
00:07:10,826 --> 00:07:14,808
[SPEAKER_00]: What I do next is a state of mind, state of uncertainty.

79
00:07:18,733 --> 00:07:25,739
[SPEAKER_00]: You can act out of craving and try to get rid of that state of uncertainty by providing answer what you should do next, or you cannot act out of craving.

80
00:07:26,539 --> 00:07:32,564
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you wonder what should you do next, you realize, well, I don't... doesn't matter what I will do, I know what I will not do.

81
00:07:33,505 --> 00:07:34,425
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you should do.

82
00:07:35,306 --> 00:07:38,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't do anything that would be along the lines of things you shouldn't be doing.

83
00:07:41,671 --> 00:07:43,332
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, so, what do I do next?

84
00:07:43,733 --> 00:07:44,233
[SPEAKER_00]: I do that.

85
00:07:44,513 --> 00:07:46,495
[SPEAKER_00]: I endure the question of what do I do next.

86
00:07:54,275 --> 00:08:07,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, the practice is just you have to constantly keep undermining that tendency to have things on your own terms, to put yourself first and then... What do I do now?

87
00:08:07,745 --> 00:08:09,426
[SPEAKER_00]: This I do, or this I don't do.

88
00:08:11,648 --> 00:08:15,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're undermining that by not giving in to finding that answer, but by enduring it.

89
00:08:18,392 --> 00:08:19,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but what do I do?

90
00:08:19,613 --> 00:08:20,354
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I do?

91
00:08:22,302 --> 00:08:24,503
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why that state of uncertainty is unpleasant.

92
00:08:24,623 --> 00:08:26,764
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, it's unpleasant only because you don't want it.

93
00:08:27,785 --> 00:08:29,566
[SPEAKER_00]: In itself, it's neither pleasant nor unpleasant.

94
00:08:31,527 --> 00:08:36,449
[SPEAKER_01]: I was saying that... that's why I said, like, it's like a state of confusion, like... Yeah, exactly.

95
00:08:36,509 --> 00:08:38,130
[SPEAKER_00]: State of confusion is real as such.

96
00:08:39,431 --> 00:08:47,935
[SPEAKER_00]: For as long as you keep trying to replace it with a state of non-confusion, you're ignoring the nature of the phenomenon as such.

97
00:08:48,663 --> 00:08:56,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means your practice, your efforts are then aimed at changing the content of that state, not understanding the nature.

98
00:08:57,106 --> 00:09:00,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's very clear that you are in the state of confusion.

99
00:09:02,208 --> 00:09:03,549
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing ambiguous about that.

100
00:09:04,709 --> 00:09:06,550
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why do you need to keep trying to define it?

101
00:09:08,130 --> 00:09:08,851
[SPEAKER_00]: State of confusion.

102
00:09:08,891 --> 00:09:09,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Confused mind.

103
00:09:09,371 --> 00:09:11,392
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows confused mind as confused mind.

104
00:09:12,172 --> 00:09:13,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-confused mind as non-confused mind.

105
00:09:14,773 --> 00:09:17,614
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, oh, he replaces confused mind with a non-confused mind.

106
00:09:18,681 --> 00:09:19,842
[SPEAKER_00]: That means he doesn't know either.

107
00:09:23,524 --> 00:09:24,945
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically going beyond doubt.

108
00:09:25,105 --> 00:09:25,766
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what doubt is.

109
00:09:25,986 --> 00:09:26,966
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a state of confusion.

110
00:09:28,267 --> 00:09:29,088
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

111
00:09:29,508 --> 00:09:29,788
[SPEAKER_00]: How?

112
00:09:31,829 --> 00:09:40,555
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you keep giving in to seeking the answer of that, you're overlooking that it is very clear as such.

113
00:09:41,015 --> 00:09:43,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Doubt is clear in itself, as a doubt.

114
00:09:43,957 --> 00:09:48,420
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can doubt this, you can doubt that, but you cannot doubt that you're experiencing doubt.

115
00:09:49,478 --> 00:09:52,140
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the level now you need to train towards.

116
00:09:54,201 --> 00:09:55,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Our state of confusion is here.

117
00:09:55,963 --> 00:09:56,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

118
00:09:56,323 --> 00:10:00,066
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the confused mind is present, enduring.

119
00:10:00,106 --> 00:10:01,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter what you're confused about.

120
00:10:02,547 --> 00:10:03,568
[SPEAKER_00]: You know what you will not do.

121
00:10:03,848 --> 00:10:05,069
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's not an issue.

122
00:10:05,529 --> 00:10:06,730
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing there to talk about.

123
00:10:07,831 --> 00:10:09,012
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to uphold that.

124
00:10:10,073 --> 00:10:13,235
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, in terms of, so, what do then I do on top of things I don't do?

125
00:10:13,355 --> 00:10:14,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's something you have to endure.

126
00:10:15,657 --> 00:10:17,338
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize, oh, that's what I have to do.

127
00:10:19,583 --> 00:10:21,424
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't do that on your own terms.

128
00:10:21,504 --> 00:10:23,845
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it can never become the answer.

129
00:10:23,865 --> 00:10:24,346
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I do?

130
00:10:24,546 --> 00:10:25,887
[SPEAKER_00]: I endure that like that.

131
00:10:26,007 --> 00:10:31,430
[SPEAKER_00]: You can even think that, but when you actually start enduring it, it cannot be done on your own terms as something you do.

132
00:10:32,130 --> 00:10:34,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why it never provides that answer that you seek.

133
00:10:34,672 --> 00:10:37,513
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's why it undermines that dependence on the answer you seek.

134
00:10:41,576 --> 00:10:45,118
[SPEAKER_00]: The whole point of practice is to endure the right things.

135
00:10:47,129 --> 00:10:52,031
[SPEAKER_00]: Anybody can endure anything, but not anybody can endure the right things that need to be endured.

136
00:10:55,052 --> 00:10:55,852
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why most people...

137
00:10:55,872 --> 00:11:03,976
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, we have insane amount of determination and commitment, and still have completely wrong views, because they're not enduring the right things.

138
00:11:11,238 --> 00:11:14,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's... that's...

139
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:15,040
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that's... that's...

140
00:11:16,637 --> 00:11:22,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty much, the other side of the coin is like, how do I know I'm doing the right thing?

141
00:11:23,581 --> 00:11:29,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, again, whatever question presents itself, don't look at the content, don't look at the answer.

142
00:11:29,985 --> 00:11:31,326
[SPEAKER_00]: How do I know am I doing the right thing?

143
00:11:31,647 --> 00:11:33,027
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the experience of that?

144
00:11:35,249 --> 00:11:38,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's a state of confusion that we just spoke about.

145
00:11:38,471 --> 00:11:42,193
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's the whole point.

146
00:11:42,233 --> 00:11:44,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Certain questions will present themselves like, you must answer this.

147
00:11:45,915 --> 00:11:50,116
[SPEAKER_00]: But you know it's fine, yeah, okay, okay, we'll give you that, all the previous questions, that's fine.

148
00:11:50,356 --> 00:11:58,057
[SPEAKER_00]: But this is the one you must... Actually, it's still a question, it's still the same nature, it's still the phenomenon of confusion, the mind of confusion.

149
00:11:58,337 --> 00:11:58,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh!

150
00:11:59,177 --> 00:12:04,798
[SPEAKER_00]: So thus, I will not answer it.

151
00:12:05,138 --> 00:12:13,400
[SPEAKER_01]: To a degree, it makes sense that I cannot make confusion, like, provide clarity out of

152
00:12:14,525 --> 00:12:15,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

153
00:12:15,345 --> 00:12:15,826
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

154
00:12:15,886 --> 00:12:27,830
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only act out of trying to get rid of that state, which means you're not doing anything in terms of understanding or overcoming the unpleasantness of that state.

155
00:12:27,990 --> 00:12:31,532
[SPEAKER_00]: You're only replacing it, which means you remain equally liable to it.

156
00:12:34,363 --> 00:12:47,871
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people who keep kind of habitually acting out of doubt, trying to get rid of the doubt, the unpleasantness of it, by finding the answer, by reclarifying the answer, by revisiting the answer, by rehashing the whole thing that they already know, they can never appease that.

157
00:12:48,712 --> 00:12:50,272
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not... it's not rational.

158
00:12:51,213 --> 00:12:53,755
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about providing the answer which you already have.

159
00:12:54,615 --> 00:12:56,976
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about removing that emotional discomfort.

160
00:12:57,537 --> 00:12:59,258
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not in your rational control.

161
00:13:00,270 --> 00:13:05,732
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're just sort of, you know, squirming, and twisting, and turning, until it hopefully disappears.

162
00:13:07,132 --> 00:13:11,293
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the more you do it, the more you... the more dependent you become on doing it.

163
00:13:12,353 --> 00:13:16,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, what used to provide the answer won't be enough, after a while.

164
00:13:17,275 --> 00:13:27,958
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's a thing like... Like, even when you're saying, like, it's not about what you do, what you don't do,

165
00:13:29,956 --> 00:13:32,357
[SPEAKER_01]: I know that's pretty much a decent answer.

166
00:13:32,377 --> 00:13:37,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, pretty much, for me, it appears to be a decent answer.

167
00:13:37,918 --> 00:13:42,239
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, there's nothing emotionally satisfactory about that.

168
00:13:42,259 --> 00:13:44,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

169
00:13:44,479 --> 00:13:57,682
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's one thing that you're kind of learning through making effort is to not trust the emotional satisfaction or dissatisfaction as a criteria for what makes sense or not.

170
00:14:00,328 --> 00:14:02,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody starts like that, basically.

171
00:14:02,469 --> 00:14:05,110
[SPEAKER_00]: What pleases you, that's what seems to be right.

172
00:14:05,850 --> 00:14:08,671
[SPEAKER_00]: What displeases you, that's what you take to be wrong.

173
00:14:10,832 --> 00:14:17,334
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is no other criteria there, which means you're judging things as right or wrong simply based on how they make you feel.

174
00:14:20,035 --> 00:14:20,955
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't want that.

175
00:14:24,396 --> 00:14:25,817
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the whole point.

176
00:14:26,217 --> 00:14:28,718
[SPEAKER_00]: If people could, effortlessly,

177
00:14:29,977 --> 00:14:36,321
[SPEAKER_00]: Judge right as right, wrong as wrong, regardless of how it makes them feel, they would effortlessly fall into the right view.

178
00:14:38,001 --> 00:14:47,787
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not incidental that you have to endure the pain and discomfort and seeming confusion in regard to what you kind of know it's right, but you cannot accept because it's so displeasing.

179
00:14:48,767 --> 00:14:53,070
[SPEAKER_00]: If you endure through that, the displeasure will fade away and then you realize, no, this is right.

180
00:14:53,270 --> 00:14:54,230
[SPEAKER_00]: I always knew this was right.

181
00:14:54,851 --> 00:14:56,912
[SPEAKER_00]: I just had to go through it and endure it.

182
00:14:59,070 --> 00:15:00,771
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why that's where the right view is.

183
00:15:01,191 --> 00:15:02,472
[SPEAKER_00]: That's going beyond the doubt.

184
00:15:03,253 --> 00:15:08,756
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't go beyond the doubt by satisfying every possible answer you can doubt and be confused about.

185
00:15:10,077 --> 00:15:15,881
[SPEAKER_00]: You go beyond the doubt by being unable anymore to resist the discomfort of doubt.

186
00:15:17,963 --> 00:15:25,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you become stronger than it doesn't matter the amount of doubt, the amount of discomfort, cannot waver your mind in regard to the nature of it.

187
00:15:26,708 --> 00:15:30,890
[SPEAKER_00]: You can still doubt the content and sometimes even clarify if needs to be clarified.

188
00:15:31,430 --> 00:15:35,612
[SPEAKER_00]: But you won't have that burden of, I must do that because I don't know any other escape from this.

189
00:15:48,777 --> 00:15:49,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

190
00:15:52,270 --> 00:15:58,912
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason that I do anything else is because it provides an idea of certainty, like... Yeah.

191
00:15:59,472 --> 00:16:02,533
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's an inevitable way of starting your practice, yeah.

192
00:16:03,253 --> 00:16:05,394
[SPEAKER_00]: But sooner or later, it's going to reach a dead end.

193
00:16:06,434 --> 00:16:20,218
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, you can either, you know, dig up a new inspiration towards something, or change a tradition, or change the monastery, or change the teacher, as people do when when their practice runs dry, or eventually disrobe.

194
00:16:22,523 --> 00:16:24,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you can realize, but it's not about inspiration.

195
00:16:24,685 --> 00:16:26,187
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about having something to do.

196
00:16:26,608 --> 00:16:29,991
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about not depending on anything that can be done.

197
00:16:30,912 --> 00:16:32,354
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you free yourself from karma.

198
00:16:32,554 --> 00:16:35,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Not by doing it, but by understanding beyond it.

199
00:16:43,422 --> 00:16:45,864
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you mean by, like, inspiration in this instance?

200
00:16:46,825 --> 00:16:50,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, like, see, like, you... you recognize the problem, as you said, like, for example, sensuality.

201
00:16:51,790 --> 00:16:53,972
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're... oh, I can work on this.

202
00:16:54,232 --> 00:16:54,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's fine.

203
00:16:55,754 --> 00:16:56,575
[SPEAKER_00]: And you work on it.

204
00:16:57,576 --> 00:17:03,421
[SPEAKER_00]: But, see, that was initially probably enough to keep you within the context of practice.

205
00:17:04,342 --> 00:17:07,145
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, a few days later, oh, what do I do next?

206
00:17:08,656 --> 00:17:11,597
[SPEAKER_00]: While I still work on the... Are you free from sensuality?

207
00:17:11,617 --> 00:17:12,818
[SPEAKER_00]: So you ask what to do next.

208
00:17:13,058 --> 00:17:13,198
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

209
00:17:14,179 --> 00:17:17,680
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet here you are asking what to do next.

210
00:17:17,820 --> 00:17:18,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is that rooted?

211
00:17:19,721 --> 00:17:27,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Rooted basically in dependence on certain extent of inspiration that now has run dry because you've been repeating the same practice of not giving in to sensuality.

212
00:17:29,106 --> 00:17:33,448
[SPEAKER_00]: So it doesn't... you don't get that same inspirational buzz from it, so to speak.

213
00:17:34,958 --> 00:17:36,739
[SPEAKER_00]: So now I quickly need to find something more.

214
00:17:36,779 --> 00:17:39,080
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I do next, while I still do this?

215
00:17:40,841 --> 00:17:42,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's very unpleasant.

216
00:17:42,402 --> 00:17:42,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

217
00:17:43,242 --> 00:17:45,664
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very unpleasant to practice without inspiration.

218
00:17:46,304 --> 00:17:47,865
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly where the practice is.

219
00:17:48,945 --> 00:17:56,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you start practicing like that, then, even on the most uninspiring days of your life, you cannot go back.

220
00:17:57,230 --> 00:17:58,571
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot, like, slip back.

221
00:17:59,411 --> 00:18:01,192
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot do things that you would regret later.

222
00:18:01,892 --> 00:18:04,654
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you stop acting out of how you feel.

223
00:18:07,898 --> 00:18:13,223
[SPEAKER_00]: But the only way to stop acting on account of how you feel, is to go through not acting on account of how you feel.

224
00:18:13,644 --> 00:18:14,244
[SPEAKER_00]: To endure it.

225
00:18:25,976 --> 00:18:27,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Because nothing has changed.

226
00:18:28,873 --> 00:18:32,456
[SPEAKER_00]: The thoughts you had when you were inspired were not, you know, very strained, sensual thoughts.

227
00:18:33,157 --> 00:18:36,299
[SPEAKER_00]: That idea, practice, context hasn't changed.

228
00:18:36,780 --> 00:18:38,541
[SPEAKER_00]: What has changed is how you feel about it.

229
00:18:40,123 --> 00:18:42,084
[SPEAKER_00]: The novelty ran out, the inspiration ran out.

230
00:18:42,685 --> 00:18:51,753
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's like, oh, so now I'm looking for what else do I do on account of basically my feelings changing, not on account of only completing what I had to do or whatever else.

231
00:18:52,654 --> 00:18:54,215
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're just acting on account of how you feel.

232
00:18:58,688 --> 00:18:59,928
[SPEAKER_00]: You're trying to avoid the discomfort.

233
00:19:16,735 --> 00:19:19,656
[SPEAKER_00]: And that also kind of ties in with what we spoke before, a while back.

234
00:19:20,656 --> 00:19:21,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you might go, what do I do?

235
00:19:21,777 --> 00:19:22,637
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I... what should I do?

236
00:19:22,657 --> 00:19:23,157
[SPEAKER_00]: What should I do?

237
00:19:23,417 --> 00:19:25,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Without realizing, you're already doing loads of stuff.

238
00:19:26,768 --> 00:19:32,210
[SPEAKER_00]: You're already sitting on the chair, walking on a meditation path, I don't know, feeding a cat.

239
00:19:32,570 --> 00:19:35,591
[SPEAKER_00]: You're already doing all... the body is already performing all these things.

240
00:19:37,111 --> 00:19:38,152
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're completely unaware of it.

241
00:19:39,772 --> 00:19:44,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the only doing that you recognize is doing on the terms of my own self.

242
00:19:45,574 --> 00:19:46,794
[SPEAKER_00]: I do this, I do that.

243
00:19:48,095 --> 00:19:49,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see that there's doing happening already.

244
00:19:50,356 --> 00:19:51,296
[SPEAKER_00]: And only... the only thing

245
00:19:52,228 --> 00:20:00,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Even when yourself does something, it's basically... yourself can only appropriate that which body does already, or the body can do already.

246
00:20:05,499 --> 00:20:14,065
[SPEAKER_01]: It's only appropriated action would come under as intention.

247
00:20:15,967 --> 00:20:16,668
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't understand.

248
00:20:18,029 --> 00:20:20,591
[SPEAKER_01]: No, the thing is that... say...

249
00:20:21,987 --> 00:20:27,469
[SPEAKER_01]: The other day, I was lying down, and I have a habit of wiggling my foot.

250
00:20:28,610 --> 00:20:30,490
[SPEAKER_01]: And I am wiggling my foot.

251
00:20:30,730 --> 00:20:33,231
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm like...

252
00:20:33,311 --> 00:20:38,253
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't see any intention of me doing that, but I'm doing that.

253
00:20:40,374 --> 00:20:42,875
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what else am I missing sort of thing?

254
00:20:43,275 --> 00:20:43,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, right.

255
00:20:44,376 --> 00:20:49,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, there are those things that you're doing kind of sub-intentionally, so to speak.

256
00:20:52,236 --> 00:20:54,858
[SPEAKER_00]: And those things are not things you necessarily need to worry about.

257
00:20:56,599 --> 00:21:00,281
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the intention determines whether the thing is going to be wholesome or unwholesome.

258
00:21:00,842 --> 00:21:02,403
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is no intention, there's no action.

259
00:21:06,205 --> 00:21:07,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Certainly no action that you should worry about.

260
00:21:08,507 --> 00:21:11,609
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you intentionally choose to, I don't know,

261
00:21:13,273 --> 00:21:16,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Wiggle your foot, because it provides you with physical pleasure, let's say.

262
00:21:17,475 --> 00:21:22,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, that's a completely different intention, a completely different action now, because it's rooted in completely different things.

263
00:21:22,597 --> 00:21:27,639
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same thing externally, but that's not what determines whether action is wholesome or unwholesome.

264
00:21:28,439 --> 00:21:31,180
[SPEAKER_00]: It's whether it's rooted in wholesome or unwholesome internally.

265
00:22:30,726 --> 00:22:44,336
[SPEAKER_01]: My responsibility towards sensuality is, whenever I see a sensual thought, not to be involved with it, or not, like, restrain myself from it.

266
00:22:44,957 --> 00:22:55,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, restrain from welcoming it, delighting in it, thinking more on account of it, towards it, letting it basically fester.

267
00:22:56,505 --> 00:22:58,166
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot prevent them from arising.

268
00:22:58,567 --> 00:22:58,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

269
00:22:59,599 --> 00:23:02,800
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can prevent them from staying by not entertaining them.

270
00:23:04,140 --> 00:23:11,741
[SPEAKER_01]: The the the underlying tendencies or whatever, it's it's it's not really my responsibility.

271
00:23:12,421 --> 00:23:14,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, they're indirect responsibilities.

272
00:23:14,622 --> 00:23:17,562
[SPEAKER_00]: See, you know that you're not free from sensuality.

273
00:23:18,682 --> 00:23:28,044
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know that simply by welcoming sensual thoughts, because you're not free from sensuality, by welcoming them, sensuality will increase.

274
00:23:29,008 --> 00:23:30,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Underlying tendencies will increase.

275
00:23:31,769 --> 00:23:41,351
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know that indirectly, by accepting this, I'm actually resulting in increasing... causing the underlying tendency to increase, which means it's going to be coming more and more often then.

276
00:23:43,532 --> 00:23:46,653
[SPEAKER_01]: But I... just because I'm restraining, I cannot expect them to dry out.

277
00:23:47,693 --> 00:23:51,334
[SPEAKER_00]: You can expect them to dry out, but you don't know when they will dry out.

278
00:23:52,182 --> 00:23:54,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you can know that, well, I'm not entertaining it.

279
00:23:55,184 --> 00:24:00,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, if I'm not entertaining it, I am not fueling whatever underlying tendencies there are.

280
00:24:01,589 --> 00:24:04,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, simply by not entertaining, you are drying them up.

281
00:24:04,852 --> 00:24:06,132
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can know that for sure.

282
00:24:07,233 --> 00:24:09,655
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just that people, like, usually want more.

283
00:24:09,735 --> 00:24:11,637
[SPEAKER_00]: They want to know how long I have to do this.

284
00:24:12,097 --> 00:24:12,978
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't know that.

285
00:24:13,718 --> 00:24:17,641
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the simile in the sutta of the the axe handle.

286
00:24:18,262 --> 00:24:20,944
[SPEAKER_00]: The carpenter uses his axe every day.

287
00:24:21,783 --> 00:24:23,144
[SPEAKER_00]: And the handle is wearing thin.

288
00:24:24,285 --> 00:24:26,146
[SPEAKER_00]: But he doesn't know how much it's wearing thin.

289
00:24:26,727 --> 00:24:27,567
[SPEAKER_00]: But he keeps using it.

290
00:24:27,607 --> 00:24:29,168
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't care how much it's wearing thin.

291
00:24:29,208 --> 00:24:30,309
[SPEAKER_00]: He's using the handle.

292
00:24:32,431 --> 00:24:37,675
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, whatever, a few years later, he comes and realizes, oh, I have to replace my handle now.

293
00:24:37,815 --> 00:24:38,655
[SPEAKER_00]: It's way too thin.

294
00:24:40,016 --> 00:24:40,657
[SPEAKER_00]: It's unusable.

295
00:24:40,997 --> 00:24:41,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Gone.

296
00:24:41,597 --> 00:24:42,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Faded away.

297
00:24:55,348 --> 00:25:07,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, don't think of the practice in general, but in this particular instance, don't think of, oh, I will do this and then to achieve like a certain goal, like freedom from centrality.

298
00:25:07,196 --> 00:25:10,558
[SPEAKER_00]: I will restrain my thoughts and then I will achieve the freedom from centrality.

299
00:25:12,580 --> 00:25:17,403
[SPEAKER_00]: You should think of it more like, I will restrain my thoughts and that's my goal.

300
00:25:20,238 --> 00:25:28,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, to develop a type... like a state of being that is about restraint of sensual thoughts, not about indulgence of sensual thoughts.

301
00:25:29,144 --> 00:25:30,025
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's your goal.

302
00:25:31,346 --> 00:25:35,529
[SPEAKER_00]: That sense restraint becomes your norm, not other way around.

303
00:25:36,950 --> 00:25:40,312
[SPEAKER_00]: That sense restraint becomes effortless, not other way around.

304
00:25:46,567 --> 00:25:56,490
[SPEAKER_00]: And if that becomes your goal, which means... if that becomes your goal, it would be impossible to not have your efforts directed towards that goal.

305
00:25:59,151 --> 00:26:05,153
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if that becomes your goal, with all of your invested effort, it would be impossible to not be free from centrality.

306
00:26:10,290 --> 00:26:25,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, in certain ways, that became the goal and the inspiration started growing out, so it's... Yeah, well, like, when... what happens is, when that becomes your goal, to be free from sensuality, well, basically, that goal is on the basis of sensuality.

307
00:26:25,533 --> 00:26:26,793
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on the basis of craving.

308
00:26:28,434 --> 00:26:38,016
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start like that, but you want to refine it further and recognize that, well, can you have freedom from sensuality without sense restraint?

309
00:26:39,076 --> 00:26:39,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

310
00:26:41,255 --> 00:26:45,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you be free from sensuality, yet not be sense-restrained?

311
00:26:46,456 --> 00:26:46,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

312
00:26:49,557 --> 00:26:52,378
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sense-restraint needs to be there either way.

313
00:26:54,278 --> 00:26:59,680
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why don't I make sense-restraint my goal, regardless of whether I'm free from sensuality or not?

314
00:27:01,280 --> 00:27:04,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense-restraint will be my mode of being for the rest of my life.

315
00:27:09,002 --> 00:27:09,302
[SPEAKER_00]: And then,

316
00:27:11,742 --> 00:27:12,422
[SPEAKER_01]: If you stick to that.

317
00:27:15,304 --> 00:27:19,986
[SPEAKER_00]: It's again, it's like the other simile, the Buddha said, the hand lies on her eggs.

318
00:27:23,648 --> 00:27:37,436
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether she wants, whether she wishes, that chicks pierce out safely and come into this world healthy and alive, or whether she doesn't wish that, they will, if she lays on her eggs, if she makes the effort.

319
00:27:39,420 --> 00:27:51,126
[SPEAKER_00]: But if she gets too busy figuring out what will she do with the chicks when they come out, and how much she'll enjoy them, and look after them, she forgets to sit on the eggs, they won't come out.

320
00:27:53,146 --> 00:27:59,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if she has all the inspiration and determination for them to come out, they won't, because she's not doing, creating the necessary basis for it.

321
00:28:00,750 --> 00:28:01,370
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

322
00:28:01,590 --> 00:28:05,112
[SPEAKER_00]: You start by wanting your chicks, but then you realize, okay, so how will that come to be?

323
00:28:05,172 --> 00:28:08,894
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start by wanting to be free from underlying tendency towards sensuality.

324
00:28:13,677 --> 00:28:16,998
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you refine that further and see how would that come to be.

325
00:28:17,038 --> 00:28:19,859
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that would have to come to be through the sense restraint.

326
00:28:22,880 --> 00:28:24,021
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's my goal now.

327
00:28:24,841 --> 00:28:36,146
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for you, from where you are at, sense restraint equals freedom from underlying tendency, prevention of underlying tendency, drying up of sensuality.

328
00:28:40,027 --> 00:28:42,508
[SPEAKER_00]: And if there is anything more within that you need to do,

329
00:28:44,533 --> 00:28:49,034
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be done again, only within sensory strain that you have to do.

330
00:28:49,614 --> 00:28:51,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Not in place of or instead of.

331
00:28:52,875 --> 00:28:55,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, you just have to start doing the sensory strain.

332
00:28:56,436 --> 00:29:03,038
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if there is anything more, it will show itself through what you are already doing.

333
00:29:07,060 --> 00:29:08,600
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it goes the same for, like, ill will.

334
00:29:09,722 --> 00:29:18,564
[SPEAKER_00]: You make your goal to become like a... have a state of being that's based on non-ill-will.

335
00:29:20,264 --> 00:29:23,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of what's in front of you and what thoughts present themselves.

336
00:29:24,465 --> 00:29:26,185
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoughts of ill-will, thoughts of hatred.

337
00:29:27,066 --> 00:29:30,626
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't welcome, you don't cherish, you don't delight in, you don't entertain it.

338
00:29:32,527 --> 00:29:33,147
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your goal.

339
00:29:35,102 --> 00:29:39,944
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to worry about what's going to come as a result of it, because you already know what will come as a result of it.

340
00:29:42,986 --> 00:29:49,089
[SPEAKER_00]: You already know that as long as you lay on the eggs, chicks will have to roost.

341
00:29:49,349 --> 00:29:50,370
[SPEAKER_00]: They will have to come out.

342
00:29:51,470 --> 00:29:52,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't think about it.

343
00:29:52,891 --> 00:29:58,294
[SPEAKER_00]: You just don't need to think about it.

344
00:30:02,496 --> 00:30:04,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, as a belief though, right?

345
00:30:06,110 --> 00:30:07,171
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not even a belief.

346
00:30:10,293 --> 00:30:23,302
[SPEAKER_00]: You already recognize on some level that sensory strain implies non-centrality, creates absence of centrality.

347
00:30:25,263 --> 00:30:30,847
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know to what extent, but you already see the thread, you already see the connection.

348
00:31:08,497 --> 00:31:10,417
[SPEAKER_01]: You said it again?

349
00:31:10,457 --> 00:31:19,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's too little to acknowledge, exactly.

350
00:31:19,139 --> 00:31:19,620
[SPEAKER_01]: You need more.

351
00:31:19,800 --> 00:31:22,840
[SPEAKER_01]: But the only reason you need more is because you've been craving for more.

352
00:31:32,697 --> 00:31:35,298
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you undo that craving, you realize, I don't need anything.

353
00:31:35,818 --> 00:31:38,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, doesn't matter how subtle it is, it's more than enough.

354
00:31:38,580 --> 00:31:41,381
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you basically stop needing any inspiration for practice.

355
00:31:45,843 --> 00:31:47,364
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you become that practice.

356
00:31:48,664 --> 00:31:49,685
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to be doing it.

357
00:32:01,640 --> 00:32:13,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you might be confused in regard to certain content or certain choices or what to do here, what to do there, but you will not be confused in regard to the nature, in regard to the order of things.

358
00:32:14,335 --> 00:32:17,999
[SPEAKER_00]: The order of things is you need to lay on the eggs for chicks to roost.

359
00:32:18,700 --> 00:32:20,402
[SPEAKER_00]: There is absolutely no other way there.

360
00:32:21,756 --> 00:32:22,777
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just how it works.

361
00:32:23,458 --> 00:32:29,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, here you are, knowing that, ignoring that, and worrying what school will you send your chicks into?

362
00:32:30,604 --> 00:32:31,525
[SPEAKER_00]: And what will they wear?

363
00:32:31,565 --> 00:32:32,346
[SPEAKER_00]: And what will they do?

364
00:32:32,406 --> 00:32:33,587
[SPEAKER_00]: And how will you teach them this?

365
00:32:34,468 --> 00:32:37,611
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, at the expense of that, you're forgetting that you need to sit on the eggs.

366
00:32:39,292 --> 00:32:40,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Because now you're getting all confused.

367
00:32:40,714 --> 00:32:41,294
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do I do?

368
00:32:41,555 --> 00:32:43,737
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start being confused even about what you knew.

369
00:32:44,417 --> 00:32:47,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you've been giving in to trying to get rid of the confusion.

370
00:32:49,802 --> 00:32:59,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see, well, doesn't matter how confused I am about how will I raise my chicks, I cannot be confused about the nature of that being present there.

371
00:33:00,210 --> 00:33:02,131
